Cell["\<\
From the graph, we can observe that:

The exact and approximate derivatives are very close to each other at almost \
all points, which indicates that the numerical approximation is quite \
accurate for this function within the examined range.

The critical points identified and classified as maxima (in green) and minima \
(in purple) help in understanding the behavior of the function. These are \
points where the function\[CloseCurlyQuote]s slope changes from increasing to \
decreasing (maxima) or from decreasing to increasing (minima).

The critical points are not only found at \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]flat\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] regions of the function (where the slope is zero) \
but also at points where the function\[CloseCurlyQuote]s graph has \
significant curvature, which is expected as these are points where the \
derivative (slope) changes sign.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9182509046721134`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"76a23104-6bf4-0e4b-be4b-\
1d8ad673028b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Comparative Analysis of Nonlinear Equation Solving Methods", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.918251930936535*^9},ExpressionUUID->"a9c91c40-cca4-9c4a-9667-\
70d17d5ccb9e"],

Cell["\<\



Solution Accuracy:

Bisection Method: With 1 iteration, the bisection method converged to a \
solution of x \[TildeTilde]  2. This solution is quite close to the true \
solution computed by Mathematica.
Newton-Raphson Method: The Newton-Raphson method took 4 iterations to \
converge to the same solution of  x \[TildeTilde] 2. Again, this result \
aligns closely with the Mathematica solution.
Secant Method: The secant method converged in 6 iterations to the solution x \
\[TildeTilde] 2, which is slightly slower compared to the bisection and \
Newton-Raphson methods but still provides an accurate result.
2. Computational Efficiency:

Bisection Method: The bisection method took 1168 microseconds to converge, \
demonstrating a relatively slower computational speed due to its nature of \
halving the interval at each step.
Newton-Raphson Method: Newton-Raphson was the fastest among the three \
methods, taking only 405 microseconds to converge. However, it requires the \
evaluation of both the function and its derivative at each iteration.
Secant Method: The secant method took 513 microseconds to converge, which is \
faster than the bisection method but slightly slower than Newton-Raphson. It \
offers a balance between computational speed and accuracy.
3. Trade-off Between Accuracy and Speed:

Newton-Raphson vs. Secant: Although the Newton-Raphson method is faster, it \
requires the computation of both the function and its derivative, which might \
be costly for complex functions. On the other hand, the secant method \
approximates the derivative using finite differences, making it \
computationally less expensive but potentially slower in convergence. The \
choice between these methods depends on the trade-off between computational \
resources and desired accuracy.
In conclusion, while the Newton-Raphson method offers the fastest \
convergence, the secant method provides a balance between computational \
efficiency and accuracy, making it a favorable choice when function \
evaluations are costly. Understanding the characteristics and trade-offs of \
each method is crucial for selecting the most suitable approach based on \
specific problem requirements and constraints.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.918251841758423*^9, 
  3.918251916790188*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7276eb2d-8f6b-4a43-a2d7-\
9863b58714d5"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Bouncing Ball", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9182526434514694`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"031d2b34-fe98-0b41-8a0f-\
e5869ad5c956"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Define", " ", "the", " ", "gravitational", " ", "constant"}], 
   "*)"}], 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"g", "=", "9.81"}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\n", 
   RowBox[{"(*", 
    RowBox[{
    "Define", " ", "the", " ", "parabola", " ", "arc", " ", "function"}], 
    "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"parabolaArc", "[", 
      RowBox[{"x_", ",", "x0_", ",", "y0_", ",", "vx0_", ",", "vy0_"}], "]"}],
      ":=", 
     RowBox[{"y0", "+", 
      RowBox[{"vy0", "*", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"x", "-", "x0"}], ")"}], "/", "vx0"}]}], "-", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{"1", "/", "2"}], ")"}], "*", "g", "*", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"(", 
          RowBox[{"x", "-", "x0"}], ")"}], "^", "2"}], "/", 
        RowBox[{"vx0", "^", "2"}]}]}]}]}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\n", 
   RowBox[{"(*", 
    RowBox[{
    "Define", " ", "the", " ", "parabolas", " ", "using", " ", "the", " ", 
     "given", " ", "bounce", " ", "data"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"parabolas", "=", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Plot", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"parabolaArc", "[", 
          RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", "1", ",", "5", ",", "10"}], "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", "0.05"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"Plot", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"parabolaArc", "[", 
          RowBox[{
          "x", ",", "0.05", ",", "2.04998", ",", "9.00562", ",", "4.48874"}], 
          "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{"x", ",", "0.05", ",", "0.140056"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"Plot", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"parabolaArc", "[", 
          RowBox[{
          "x", ",", "0.140056", ",", "2.1396", ",", "4.11942", ",", 
           "8.06491"}], "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{"x", ",", "0.140056", ",", "6.81353"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"Plot", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"parabolaArc", "[", 
          RowBox[{"x", ",", "6.81353", ",", "2.50583", ",", 
           RowBox[{"-", "6.33712"}], ",", "4.68787"}], "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", 
